"""Author the original, course-aligned practice bank; no student records."""
import json
import random
from pathlib import Path

items = []
topic = ""
def q(title, stem, options, correct, explanation, code="", stdout=None):
    answer = [correct] if isinstance(correct, int) else correct
    item = dict(id=len(items)+1, topic=topic, title=title, stem=stem,
                options=options, correct=answer, explanation=explanation, code=code)
    if stdout is not None:
        item["code_stdout"] = stdout
    items.append(item)

topic="Prediction foundations"
q("Response versus predictor", "In a salary prediction problem, which variable is the response?", ["Salary being predicted", "Every measured characteristic", "The random seed", "The validation fraction"],0,"The response is the quantity to predict; predictors supply information used for that prediction.")
q("What supervised learning needs", "Which are necessary ingredients of an ordinary supervised training dataset? Select all that apply.", ["Observed responses for training examples", "Predictors for those examples", "Proof that each predictor causes the response", "A response column in every future prediction request"],[0,1],"Supervised fitting uses paired predictors and responses. Causal identification is not required for prediction, and future responses are typically unknown.")
q("Regression or classification", "Which task is naturally a regression problem?", ["Predict tomorrow's measured electricity demand", "Predict whether a customer defaults", "Predict one of three species labels", "Predict whether a tumor is benign or malignant"],0,"A numerical response on a meaningful quantitative scale calls for regression; the other tasks have categorical responses.")
q("The prediction objective", "What is the main purpose of a held-out prediction assessment?", ["Estimate performance on observations not used for fitting", "Prove all coefficients are causal", "Make training error zero", "Guarantee future performance under any population change"],0,"Held-out assessment checks generalization under the evaluation design and relevant population assumptions; distribution changes can invalidate that extrapolation.")
q("Flexibility", "Which changes can generally increase effective model flexibility? Select all that apply.",["Allow deeper trees","Use a larger ridge penalty","Add boosting rounds at a fixed learning rate","Force every regression coefficient to zero"],[0,2],"Greater depth and more boosting rounds enlarge what the fit can express. A larger ridge penalty generally constrains fitting.")
q("Bias and variance", "A more flexible model reduces squared bias but increases variance. When can its expected squared prediction error fall?", ["When the bias reduction exceeds the variance increase", "Whenever training error falls", "Only when irreducible noise is zero", "Never"],0,"Under the usual squared-error decomposition, the net change depends on squared bias plus prediction variance; lower training error alone does not settle it.")
q("Irreducible error", "In Y = f(X) + noise, which component cannot be eliminated merely by estimating f more accurately from the same predictors?",["Unpredictable noise conditional on X","Estimation error in f","A coding error in preprocessing","An overly shallow chosen tree"],0,"The conditional noise component remains even if the regression function is known. More informative predictors could change the conditioning information.")
q("Generalization gap", "Training MSE is 1 and validation MSE is 12. Which conclusion is justified?",["This fitted model performs much worse on this validation set than on its training set","The model is proven fraudulent","Every deeper model must do worse","Validation labels should be added to training predictions before scoring"],0,"The observed gap is evidence to investigate overfitting, distribution differences and evaluation design. It is not a proof about every alternative model.")
q("Fixed prediction rule", "What does this prediction code return?",["[3, 7, 7]","[3, 3, 7]","[7, 7, 7]","[2, 4, 8]"],0,"The threshold uses x < 4, so x=4 goes to the second branch.","xs = [2, 4, 8]\npred = [3 if x < 4 else 7 for x in xs]\nprint(pred)","[3, 7, 7]\n")
q("The training mean baseline", "For squared-error prediction with no predictors, which constant minimizes training SSE for responses 2, 4, 8, 10?",["6","4","8","0"],0,"The SSE-minimizing constant is the arithmetic mean: (2+4+8+10)/4 = 6.")

topic="Validation and metrics"
q("Separate data roles", "Which statements about training, validation and test data are correct? Select all that apply.",["Training data fit model parameters","Validation data can guide hyperparameter choices","Repeatedly tuning against a test set preserves its untouched status","Test labels should determine the training-set scaler"],[0,1],"Validation supports selection. A test set loses its independent final-evaluation role if it repeatedly guides selection or preprocessing.")
q("Cross-validation folds", "In ordinary five-fold cross-validation, how is each candidate's validation score formed?",["Fit on four folds and score the omitted fold, rotating through all five","Fit once on all five folds and score the same fitted data","Always train on only the first fold","Choose the fold with the lowest error and discard the others"],0,"Each fold serves as held-out data once; combine the fold scores according to the chosen evaluation rule.")
q("Leakage-safe scaling", "Where should a learned standardization step be fitted during cross-validation?",["Inside each fold using only that fold's training rows","Once on the complete dataset before CV","On the final test set","On predictors and responses combined"],0,"Fitting preprocessing inside each training fold prevents held-out rows from influencing the transformation.")
q("Selecting B", "Validation MSE for 25, 100, 300 and 600 boosting trees is 15, 10, 13 and 18. Which candidate minimizes it?",["100 trees","600 trees","25 trees","300 trees"],0,"Among the candidates, 100 trees has the lowest validation MSE, regardless of whether training error keeps falling.")
q("Mean squared error", "What value is printed?",["2.5","5.0","1.5","2.0"],0,"The errors are -1 and 2, so mean squared error is (1+4)/2 = 2.5.","y = [1, 4]\npred = [2, 2]\nmse = sum((a-b)**2 for a,b in zip(y,pred)) / len(y)\nprint(mse)","2.5\n")
q("RMSE units", "If the response is measured in dollars, what are the units of RMSE and MSE?",["RMSE: dollars; MSE: dollars squared","Both dollars squared","Both dimensionless","RMSE: dollars squared; MSE: dollars"],0,"Squaring errors squares the units; taking the square root returns RMSE to the response's units.")
q("MAE and extreme errors", "Compared with MAE, why does MSE usually react more strongly to very large errors?",["Squaring gives large absolute errors disproportionate weight","MSE discards large errors","MAE always squares errors first","MSE measures only error signs"],0,"An error of magnitude 10 contributes 100 to SSE but 10 to absolute-error loss.")
q("Negative held-out R-squared", "A model has negative held-out R-squared, defined against the held-out response mean. What does that mean?",["Its held-out SSE exceeds that mean-only benchmark's SSE","Every fitted prediction is negative","Its training R-squared must also be negative","The score must be a programming error"],0,"R-squared = 1 - SSE/SST can be negative out of sample. If held-out responses are constant, this denominator is zero and the ratio needs special handling.")
q("Time-ordered data", "You want to forecast next month's sales. What is a key risk of randomly mixing future and past rows across training and validation?",["Future information can influence evaluation of predictions supposedly made earlier","Random splitting always produces too many rows","MSE becomes undefined","A chronological split makes leakage impossible in every pipeline"],0,"Forecast validation must respect information available at the prediction date; preprocessing and lag construction must also avoid future leakage.")
q("Validation uncertainty", "Why can a validation error computed on six observations vary substantially across random splits?",["The small held-out sample can give a noisy performance estimate","Six observations guarantee unbiased hyperparameter selection","The random seed removes sampling uncertainty","Increasing tree depth automatically fixes evaluation uncertainty"],0,"A small validation sample can be sensitive to which observations it contains. A fixed seed gives reproducibility, not a precise population estimate.")

topic="Regression and regularization"
q("OLS objective", "What does ordinary least squares minimize for the supplied training data?",["Sum of squared residuals","Sum of absolute coefficients","Misclassification rate","The largest predicted response"],0,"OLS chooses coefficients to minimize the training sum of squared differences between responses and linear predictions.")
q("Ridge penalty", "For the objective SSE + lambda*sum(beta_j^2), what does increasing a nonnegative lambda generally do?",["Strengthen coefficient shrinkage","Weaken the penalty","Guarantee every coefficient is exactly zero","Guarantee smaller validation error"],0,"A larger ridge penalty discourages coefficient magnitude more strongly; improved validation error is an empirical question.")
q("Lasso and ridge", "Which statements about L1 and L2 regularization are correct? Select all that apply.",["Lasso can set coefficients exactly to zero","Ordinary ridge generally shrinks without exact-zero selection","Either method guarantees recovery of the causal predictors","Both automatically fix response leakage"],[0,1],"The L1 penalty can produce exact zeros; L2 usually produces continuous shrinkage. Neither gives those causal or pipeline guarantees.")
q("Elastic net", "Which method combines L1-like selection and L2-like shrinkage?",["Elastic net","Unpenalized OLS","A regression stump","A training mean"],0,"Elastic net includes both L1 and L2 components, with a mixing parameter controlling their balance.")
q("Why standardize", "Why can predictor scaling affect ridge or lasso predictions if the raw predictors are not standardized?",["The penalty depends on coefficient magnitudes, which depend on measurement units","The response must become categorical","Scaling changes the number of observations","OLS and penalized regression never use coefficients"],0,"Changing a predictor's units changes the coefficient needed for the same fitted contribution, altering the penalty unless the fitting convention accounts for scale.")
q("Unpenalized rescaling", "For a full-rank OLS model, replace x by x/1000 and transform new inputs consistently. What happens?",["The corresponding slope multiplies by 1000 while fitted predictions can remain unchanged","The slope and predictions must both remain numerically unchanged","Predictions must multiply by 1000","OLS becomes lasso"],0,"The coefficient adjusts to preserve the linear contribution: beta*x = (1000*beta)*(x/1000).")
q("Standardized coefficient units", "A predictor was standardized by dividing by its original SD of 2 hours. Its fitted coefficient is 2.2. Holding other predictors fixed, what prediction change corresponds to one additional original hour?",["1.1 response units","2.2 response units","4.4 response units","0.5 response units"],0,"One original hour is half a standardized unit, giving 2.2/2 = 1.1 response units.")
q("High-dimensional fitting", "When there are more predictors than training observations, which statement is generally correct?",["Unpenalized coefficients need not be uniquely identified; regularization can constrain the fit","OLS always uniquely recovers every coefficient","The predictors must all be unrelated","Validation is no longer useful"],0,"The design cannot have full column rank when p exceeds n in the usual unrestricted linear model; penalties can supply additional structure.")
q("A linear prediction", "What does this code print?",["7.0","5.0","9.0","-1.0"],0,"The intercept contributes 1; the two terms contribute 8 and -2, for a total of 7.","intercept = 1.0\nbeta = [2.0, -0.5]\nx = [4.0, 4.0]\nprint(intercept + sum(b*v for b,v in zip(beta,x)))","7.0\n")
q("Penalty tuning", "Which claims about regularization are justified? Select all that apply.",["A suitable penalty can reduce prediction variance","Some increase in bias can accompany shrinkage","The largest possible penalty always minimizes test error","The penalty should be selected solely to minimize training SSE"],[0,1],"Shrinkage often trades increased bias for reduced variance. Validation or CV can assess the prediction tradeoff.")

topic="Classification and logistic models"
q("Log odds", "In logistic regression, which quantity is linear in predictors?",["log(p/(1-p))","p squared","The class label itself in every observation","1/p"],0,"The linear predictor models log odds; the logistic function maps it to a probability between zero and one.")
q("Odds at one half", "What are the odds when the fitted probability is 0.5?",["1","0.5","2","0"],0,"Odds = p/(1-p) = 0.5/0.5 = 1.")
q("Odds ratio", "A logistic regression coefficient is log(2). Holding other predictors fixed, what happens to the odds for a one-unit increase in its predictor?",["They multiply by 2","The probability always doubles","The odds increase by exactly 2 probability points","The response becomes continuous"],0,"A coefficient beta corresponds to an odds multiplier exp(beta); this does not imply a constant probability change.")
q("Likelihood factors", "Independent observations have probabilities of class 1 equal to 0.8, 0.25 and 0.4, with observed labels 1, 0 and 1. What is their joint likelihood?",["0.24","0.08","0.09","1.95"],0,"Multiply probabilities of the observed labels: 0.8*(1-0.25)*0.4 = 0.24.")
q("Classification threshold", "Predicted probabilities of class 1 are 0.2, 0.5 and 0.8. Using the rule predict 1 when p >= 0.5, what labels result?",["[0, 1, 1]","[0, 0, 1]","[1, 1, 1]","[0.2, 0.5, 0.8]"],0,"The specified rule includes the equality case p=0.5 in class 1.")
q("Precision", "A classifier has 8 true positives and 2 false positives. What is precision for the positive class?",["0.8","0.2","4.0","Cannot be computed without true negatives"],0,"Precision = TP/(TP+FP) = 8/10 = 0.8.")
q("Recall", "A classifier has 8 true positives and 12 false negatives. What is recall for the positive class?",["0.4","0.6","0.8","1.5"],0,"Recall = TP/(TP+FN) = 8/20 = 0.4.")
q("Accuracy with imbalance", "A dataset has 99% class 0 observations. A model predicts class 0 for everyone. Which are true? Select all that apply.",["Its accuracy is 99%","Its recall for class 1 is zero if class 1 occurs","Its 99% accuracy proves usefulness for detecting class 1","It correctly identifies every positive case"],[0,1],"An imbalanced majority classifier can have high accuracy while failing to detect any examples of the minority class.")
q("Confident mistakes", "For an observed class-1 response, which predicted probability gives the largest log loss?",["0.01","0.25","0.75","0.99"],0,"For y=1, log loss is -log(p). Confidently assigning a very small probability to the true class is heavily penalized.")
q("Label versus probability", "What is printed?",["[0, 1, 1]","[1, 0, 0]","[0, 0, 1]","[0.2, 0.5, 0.8]"],0,"The boolean comparison is converted to an integer: False becomes 0 and True becomes 1.","probabilities = [0.2, 0.5, 0.8]\nprint([int(p >= 0.5) for p in probabilities])","[0, 1, 1]\n")

topic="Decision trees"
q("Regression leaf", "A regression tree leaf contains training responses 2, 4 and 9. Under squared-error fitting, what does the leaf predict?",["5","4","9","15"],0,"The leaf predicts the mean: (2+4+9)/3 = 5.")
q("Choosing a split", "A regression-tree split is evaluated under squared error. Which criterion is used?",["The reduction in within-node response SSE","The alphabetical order of feature names","The number of positive residuals alone","The height of the tallest observation"],0,"CART compares candidate partitions through the resulting within-child sums of squared response deviations.")
q("A useful split", "A node has responses [1,1,9,9]. A candidate split separates the first two from the last two. What is total child SSE?",["0","64","128","16"],0,"Both children contain identical responses, so deviations from each child's mean are zero.")
q("Depth-one trees", "What is a decision stump?",["A tree with one split and two terminal regions","A tree with no limit on depth","An average of 100 deep trees","A penalized linear regression"],0,"A stump has a root split and two terminal regions. It is often used as a weak learner.")
q("Depth convention", "Here, maximum depth counts splits from root to leaf. What is the maximum number of leaves in a binary tree of depth 3?",["8","3","6","16"],0,"A full binary tree can double the number of nodes at each level, giving at most 2^3 = 8 leaves.")
q("Minimum leaf size", "Increasing a regression tree's minimum leaf size generally does what?",["Restricts small terminal groups and can reduce flexibility","Guarantees lower training error","Forces every leaf to have exactly that many rows","Adds more candidate predictors"],0,"The constraint prevents leaves below the threshold; it does not require every leaf to have exactly the minimum count.")
q("Tree instability", "Why can a single deep tree have high prediction variance?",["Small training-data changes can alter early splits and many downstream regions","Its predictions are always linear","It cannot fit interactions","Its leaf predictions never depend on training responses"],0,"Recursive split choices can be unstable: a change near the top can alter a large part of the tree.")
q("Pruning", "What is the purpose of complexity-penalized tree pruning?",["Trade training fit against tree complexity","Guarantee the largest possible tree","Remove the response column from validation data","Make every prediction exactly zero"],0,"Pruning selects among subtrees by balancing fit and size; the appropriate complexity level can be selected with validation or CV.")
q("Tree prediction code", "What does this stump predict for x=4?",["9","3","4","6"],0,"The left branch includes only x<=2.5. Since 4 is larger, the right leaf returns 9.","def stump(x):\n    return 3 if x <= 2.5 else 9\nprint(stump(4))","9\n")
q("Extrapolation", "For an ordinary regression tree with constant terminal predictions, what happens far beyond the largest training value of one predictor, holding other inputs fixed?",["Prediction follows the reached terminal region's constant value","Prediction must continue a fitted straight-line trend","Prediction necessarily diverges to infinity","The tree estimates a new slope from future labels"],0,"Constant-leaf trees do not automatically extrapolate a linear trend outside their observed predictor range.")

topic="Bagging and random forests"
q("Bootstrap sampling", "What defines an ordinary n-row bootstrap sample from n training rows?",["Draw n times with replacement","Select all rows exactly once","Draw n times without replacement","Draw only validation rows"],0,"A bootstrap sample can repeat some training observations and omit others.")
q("Bagging prediction", "For regression, how are ordinary bagged tree predictions combined?",["Average them","Subtract each prediction from the next","Always take the maximum","Fit later trees only to residuals"],0,"Bagging fits predictors on bootstrap samples and averages their regression predictions.")
q("Why averaging helps", "When can averaging noisy predictors reduce variance substantially?",["When their errors are not perfectly correlated","Only when every predictor is identical","When all trees use validation responses as inputs","Only if each individual predictor is unbiased in every finite sample"],0,"Averaging benefits from error diversity; perfectly correlated fluctuations cannot be averaged away.")
q("Forest randomization", "Besides bootstrap rows, what randomization distinguishes a standard random forest from ordinary bagging?",["A subset of candidate predictors is considered at each split","Every tree uses a random response variable unrelated to y","Predictions receive a negative learning rate","The response is removed before fitting"],0,"Random candidate-feature subsets encourage differences among trees, especially when a strong predictor would otherwise dominate early splits.")
q("Candidate predictors", "A forest has 16 available predictors and mtry=4. At a split, what does mtry specify?",["Four candidate predictors are offered to the split search","Exactly four leaves are required","Only four trees can be fitted","Four validation rows train the split"],0,"It controls candidate predictors at each node, not tree count or leaf count.")
q("OOB eligibility", "For a given training observation, which trees may contribute to its out-of-bag prediction?",["Only trees whose bootstrap sample omitted it","Only trees that sampled it twice","Every tree regardless of its sample","Only trees chosen using that observation's error"],0,"OOB prediction excludes trees that trained on the observation itself.")
q("OOB coverage", "At an early forest stage, a training row has not been omitted by any fitted tree. How should its OOB prediction be treated?",["Unavailable at that stage","Exactly zero","Equal to its observed response","A perfectly correct prediction"],0,"There is no eligible tree vote for that row. Coverage should be reported rather than inventing a prediction.")
q("Expected omitted fraction", "For large n, approximately what fraction of original rows are omitted from an n-draw bootstrap sample?",["36.8%","0%","63.2%","100%"],0,"The omission probability is (1-1/n)^n, approaching exp(-1), about 0.368. About 63.2% appear at least once.")
q("An average of tree votes", "What is printed?",["6.0","18","9","3"],0,"The forest average is (3+6+9)/3 = 6.","tree_predictions = [3, 6, 9]\nprint(sum(tree_predictions) / len(tree_predictions))","6.0\n")
q("More forest trees", "Which statement most accurately contrasts increasing forest tree count with increasing boosting rounds?",["Forest count mainly improves approximation of its randomized-tree average; boosting rounds can increase fit flexibility and overfit","Both always monotonically improve validation error","More forest trees force later trees to fit residuals","Boosting rounds have no effect on flexibility"],0,"Forests average draws from a randomized fitting procedure; boosting sequentially adds corrections. Finite validation paths can fluctuate for either.")

topic="Gradient boosting"
q("Residual target", "Under squared-error gradient boosting, what response trains the next tree?",["Current signed residuals y-F(x)","Original y regardless of the current fit","Absolute residuals only","Current predictions F(x)"],0,"The next tree approximates the signed portion of the response left unexplained by the current ensemble.")
q("The update rule", "Which is the squared-error boosting prediction update?",["F_new(x)=F_old(x)+learning_rate*h(x)","F_new(x)=F_old(x)/h(x)","F_new(x)=mean of all original labels and x","F_new(x)=h(x) with the earlier fit discarded"],0,"The tree contributes a scaled correction to the existing predictor.")
q("A signed correction", "Actual y=5, current prediction=8, new tree output=-2 and learning rate=0.1. What is the updated prediction?",["7.8","8.2","6","-0.2"],0,"8+0.1*(-2)=7.8. The tree supplies the correction's sign; the learning rate remains positive.")
q("Learning-rate sign", "Which claims are correct? Select all that apply.",["A negative residual can produce a negative tree correction","The course learning rate is positive","Every negative residual requires a negative learning rate","A learning rate of zero would add the full tree output"],[0,1],"Signed corrections allow movements in either direction. A positive learning rate controls their size.")
q("Smaller steps", "At a fixed modest number of trees, reducing the learning rate can have which consequence?",["The model may underfit because corrections accumulate more slowly","Training error must immediately become zero","The same fit is always obtained","Interactions are automatically removed"],0,"A smaller step size generally needs more rounds to make a comparable cumulative adjustment.")
q("Sequential dependence", "Why are boosting trees not independent fits to the original training response?",["Each tree's target depends on the current ensemble's residuals","Each tree ignores the earlier fit","The targets must be bootstrap row indices","The response is never used"],0,"The residual target changes as earlier corrections change the current predictor.")
q("Boosting stumps", "Boosting uses only depth-one trees on original predictors. Which are possible? Select all that apply.",["Nonlinear single-feature effects","An additive sum of coordinate functions","Arbitrary cross-feature interactions without feature engineering","Only one predictor used across the entire ensemble"],[0,1],"Different stumps can split different variables and thresholds, producing nonlinear additive functions; summing them does not create original-feature interactions.")
q("Two residuals", "What does the code print?",["[3, -3]","[-3, 3]","[7, 7]","[10, 4]"],0,"Residual is actual minus fitted, giving 10-7 and 4-7.","y = [10, 4]\nfitted = [7, 7]\nprint([a-b for a,b in zip(y,fitted)])","[3, -3]\n")
q("Two-tree update", "Starting from F0=6, tree outputs at x are -3 and -1.5. Each receives learning rate 0.5. What is F2(x)?",["3.75","1.5","4.5","8.25"],0,"6+0.5*(-3)+0.5*(-1.5)=3.75; both scaled contributions remain in the ensemble.")
q("Why gradient", "For loss (y-F)^2/2, what is the negative derivative with respect to F?",["y-F","F-y","(y-F)^2","y+F"],0,"The derivative is F-y, so its negative is the signed residual y-F. Other losses generally produce different pseudo-residuals.")

topic="Stochastic boosting and tuning"
q("Subsample size", "There are 200 training rows and subsample fraction 0.5. This app samples without replacement for a stochastic boosting round. How many rows train that tree?",["100","200","50","400"],0,"The sampled count is 0.5*200 = 100; the subset is drawn afresh each round.")
q("Different sampling rules", "Which statements about bootstrap and without-replacement samples are correct? Select all that apply.",["A stochastic boosting subset drawn without replacement contains no duplicate row IDs within a round","A forest bootstrap sample can repeat row IDs","An observation cannot appear in two different boosting rounds","Bootstrap sampling and subsampling without replacement are identical"],[0,1],"Within-round duplication distinguishes these sampling rules. Across boosting rounds, rows can be selected repeatedly.")
q("Updating unsampled rows", "After a stochastic boosting tree is fitted, where is its correction applied when evaluating the new ensemble?",["At every input where the ensemble is evaluated, including unsampled rows","Only at sampled row IDs forever","Only at validation labels","Only to the response column"],0,"Sampling limits which rows fit the tree; it does not restrict the resulting prediction function to those row IDs.")
q("Joint tuning", "Why should learning rate and number of boosting trees be considered together?",["Smaller contributions generally need more rounds to accumulate useful fitting","They always have equal numeric values","Tree count cancels the learning rate exactly for all data","Only the learning rate affects prediction"],0,"The two controls interact; there is no universal exact equivalence between a learning-rate reduction and a particular tree-count increase.")
q("Early stopping", "Patience is 3. The rule resets patience only after sufficient validation improvement. What triggers stopping?",["Three consecutive rounds without sufficient validation improvement","The third tree regardless of scores","Any training error improvement","Three predictors having nonzero importance"],0,"Patience tracks the stated validation criterion, not a fixed tree count or training-fit condition.")
q("Retained stopping path", "A run stops after patience rounds but retains all trees fitted up to stopping. Which stage necessarily has the lowest recorded validation error?",["The stage selected by explicitly minimizing the recorded validation path","Always the final retained stage","Always stage 1","Always the deepest tree"],0,"A retained path can include rounds after its best validation stage. Restoring or selecting the best stage is a separate choice.")
q("Validation influences selection", "With validation-based early stopping enabled, which is correct?",["Validation labels can affect the selected tree count, while tree splits still use only training labels","Validation labels become ordinary tree training responses","The validation score remains an untouched final test result","Validation labels cannot affect any aspect of the fitted procedure"],0,"Selection based on validation outcomes can affect how long training continues. That differs from using those rows to fit tree splits or leaves.")
q("Same seed", "What does using the same random seed provide?",["Reproducibility for a specified implementation and configuration","Identical predictions across all software implementations","Proof that the split is representative","Removal of statistical uncertainty"],0,"Different RNGs, sampling conventions or implementations can produce different fits even when their seed numbers match.")
q("Sampling code", "Which expression samples five distinct elements without replacement from a list called rows using a random.Random instance rng?",["rng.sample(rows, 5)","rng.choices(rows, k=5)","rows * 5","sorted(rows)[:0]"],0,"random.sample samples without replacement; random.choices samples with replacement.","import random\nrng = random.Random(4710)\nrows = list(range(20))\n# Choose an expression to create the subset.")
q("Fair model comparison", "Which conditions help make two validation scores a matched comparison? Select all that apply.",["Same response and observation population","Same held-out row membership","A different test population for each setting without acknowledging it","One model's training score compared to the other's validation score"],[0,1],"Holding data and evaluation rows fixed helps isolate the effect of the modeling change. It still does not make repeated tuning an independent final test.")

topic="Interpretation and diagnostics"
q("Impurity importance", "What does impurity-based tree importance summarize?",["Training split improvements credited to each predictor","The causal effect of intervening on each predictor","Each predictor's p-value","The raw predictor mean"],0,"The measure credits reductions in the split criterion. It can be affected by correlation and the available split opportunities.")
q("Permutation importance", "For a fixed fitted model, held-out permutation importance shuffles one predictor and measures what?",["The change in held-out prediction error","The change in causal treatment effect","The error after fitting a new model to shuffled labels","The predictor's raw variance only"],0,"The fitted model is kept fixed; the shuffle disrupts information carried by that feature in the chosen evaluation sample.")
q("Negative importance", "A held-out permutation causes MSE to fall slightly. How should the measured importance be reported?",["As a negative measured error increase, with uncertainty and sample limitations considered","Clipped to a large positive number","As proof of a negative causal effect","As a logically impossible result"],0,"Finite samples and imperfect fitted models can yield negative permutation importance. The sign is not a causal-effect sign.")
q("Correlated predictors", "Why can permutation importance be difficult to interpret with highly correlated predictors?",["Other predictors may substitute for the shuffled predictor, and shuffled combinations may be unrealistic","Correlation makes every model causal","Importance becomes equal to a regression coefficient","Correlation forces all tree splits to be identical"],0,"Redundant information and off-support feature combinations complicate the isolated shuffling experiment.")
q("Partial dependence", "For a numeric feature, what does a one-feature partial-dependence curve average?",["Model predictions with that feature substituted and the other features taken from reference rows","Only observed responses with exactly that feature value","The fitted coefficient's p-value","Only the largest tree prediction"],0,"PDP is a model-based average over a specified reference distribution, not automatically an observed conditional mean or causal response curve.")
q("ICE curves", "What does each individual conditional expectation curve hold fixed?",["One reference row's other predictors while varying the feature of interest","All labels at zero","Every predictor at the full-sample mean for every curve","Only the model's random seed"],0,"Each ICE curve follows the same fitted model for one reference row as the selected predictor is varied.")
q("PDP and ICE", "If PDP and ICE use exactly the same reference rows and grid, what relationship holds?",["The PDP equals the pointwise average of those ICE curves","PDP must equal every individual ICE curve","PDP is their pointwise maximum","ICE curves cannot be used for regression"],0,"By construction, PDP averages the substituted-input predictions represented by the reference ICE curves.")
q("A median slice", "The app plots predictions while holding all other predictors at their training medians. What is this?",["A conditional input slice, generally different from PDP","Automatically the same as averaging over all training rows","A causal effect estimate","An OOB estimate"],0,"Evaluating at one median-valued vector differs from averaging over a distribution of reference rows.")
q("Centering an ICE curve", "What does this centering code print?",["[0, 3, 7]","[5, 8, 12]","[0, 8, 12]","[-5, -8, -12]"],0,"Subtracting the prediction at the reference grid point centers that ICE curve at zero there. Later values describe fitted changes from that reference.","predictions = [5, 8, 12]\nreference = predictions[0]\nprint([value-reference for value in predictions])","[0, 3, 7]\n")
q("Model explanations", "Which claims are justified by predictive importance or a fitted response plot alone? Select all that apply.",["They can describe how a fitted model uses predictors","They can motivate further diagnostic checks","They prove that changing the predictor will change an individual's outcome by the plotted amount","They eliminate confounding by construction"],[0,1],"Prediction explanations describe a fitted rule. Causal claims require additional design, assumptions and evidence.")

topic="Python and model code"
q("Prediction vector length", "What is printed?",["3","2","6","An exception"],0,"The comprehension creates one prediction for each of the three input rows.","X = [[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 6]]\npredictions = [sum(row) for row in X]\nprint(len(predictions))","3\n")
q("Repeated bootstrap rows", "What does this bootstrap-sample inspection print?",["4 3","4 4","3 4","3 3"],0,"There are four draws but only three distinct row IDs because row1 appears twice. Bootstrap samples may repeat observations.","sampled_rows = [1, 1, 4, 7]\nprint(len(sampled_rows), len(set(sampled_rows)))","4 3\n")
